{"key":"att_ger_captured_cheiroballistra","land_unit":"att_ger_captured_cheiroballistra","name":"Captured Cheiroballistra","culture":"german","campaign":"main","category":"Siege Engine","class":"Field Artillery","caste":"Light","group":"Scorpio","card":"att_rom_cheiroballistra","sources":["garrison"],"men":60,"mounts":0,"cost":785,"upkeep":30,"turns":2,"campaign_cap":0,"stats":{"melee_attack":14,"melee_defence":14,"shield_defence":14,"charge_bonus":8,"weapon_damage":15,"weapon_ap_damage":14,"bonus_v_infantry":0,"bonus_v_cavalry":20,"armour":2,"shield_armour":17,"missile_block":40,"morale":30,"hit_points":82,"mount_hit_points":0,"speed":4.1,"accuracy":0},"abilities":[{"name":"Barrage","text":"Loose projectiles more frequently for an extended period of time."}],"attributes":[{"name":"Cannot Run","text":"This unit cannot run and will only move at walking pace."}],"short":"It doesn't matter who owned it first, only who owns it now.","history":"At close targets a 'ballista' could be aimed accurately, but at a cost in range; it could only fire around 500 metres. The machine used two torsion springs with levers inserted into twisted ropes made from animal sinew, giving incredible power for its size. Firing either solid shot or bolts, it was equally effective against infantry or cavalry, but could also be deployed to destroy light fortifications during sieges. Originally developed by the Greeks around 400BC, and based on the oversized 'oxybeles' composite bow, ballistae were widely used by many cultures of the period, including the Romans. Later Roman versions included adjustable caps that enabled crews to quickly alter the tension of each sinew bundle to provide balance and therefore improve accuracy.","strengths":["Very low damage and armour penetration","Very weak in melee","Very poor morale"],"factions":[{"key":"att_fact_alamanni","name":"Alamans"},{"key":"att_fact_angli","name":"Angles"},{"key":"att_fact_bastarnae","name":"Bastarnians"},{"key":"att_fact_burgundii","nam